.TH GRFSH 1 2026-10-17 "grfsh 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
grfsh \- a small Unix shell with bash style scripting and typed table pipelines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grfsh
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RI [ script " [" args ...]]
.br
.B grfsh
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ name " [" args ...]]
.br
.B grfsh
.B \-\-check
.IR script ...
.br
.B grfsh
.B \-\-explain
.IR command " | " \-f " " script
.br
.B grfsh
.RB [ \-\-agent ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.B \-c
.I command
.br
.B grfsh
.B \-\-serve
.I socket
.br
.B grfsh
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.B \-\-client
.I socket
.RB [ \-t
.IR seconds ]
.I command
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B grfsh
reads commands from a terminal, a script file or a string and runs them.
Its syntax follows bash for the common cases: pipelines, redirections on any
file descriptor, conditionals, loops,
.BR case ,
functions, indexed and associative arrays, parameter expansion operators,
arithmetic, brace expansion, here documents, process substitution and job
control.
.PP
Interactive sessions add autosuggestions from history, syntax highlighting,
context aware completion, a theme engine for the prompt, abbreviations, key
bindings and a
.B z
command for frequently used directories.
.PP
Table commands such as
.BR files ,
.BR procs ,
.BR load ,
.BR where " and " sort\-by
pass typed rows to each other and print plain text when their output goes to
any other program.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " command"
Run
.I command
and exit. The next argument, if given, becomes
.BR $0 ,
and the rest become the positional parameters.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Act as a login shell and read the profile file before the rc file. A shell
started with an argv[0] that begins with a dash does the same.
.TP
.B \-i
Run interactively even when standard input is not a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-\-check " script" ...
Parse each script without running it and report syntax errors, unsupported
bash features and unknown commands with their line numbers. Exits with status
1 when errors are found.
.TP
.BI \-\-explain " command"
Print each command of
.I command
after expansion, with globs resolved, without running anything. Command
substitutions are left unexpanded. Notes point out deletions, force pushes,
overwritten files and variables that expand to nothing.
.B \-f
.I script
explains a file.
.TP
.B \-\-agent
Agent mode for programs that drive the shell. Pagers become
.BR cat ,
colors and git prompts are turned off, standard input is
.I /dev/null
when it would be a terminal, and commands run without a controlling
terminal. Output longer than
.B GRFSH_OUTPUT_LINES
lines keeps its first and last lines and the full text is saved to a private
file whose path is printed. Background jobs write to log files.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Agent mode that prints one JSON object with the status, standard output,
standard error, duration, final directory and changed environment variables.
.TP
.BI \-\-serve " socket"
Serve an agent mode shell on a Unix socket with mode 0600. Each line is a
command or a JSON object with
.BR command ,
.B timeout
and
.B id
fields, and each reply is a JSON line. State carries over between requests,
which run one at a time with standard input from
.IR /dev/null .
SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGINT remove the socket before the server exits.
.B exit
stops the server.
.TP
.BI \-\-client " socket"
Send one command to a server and print its output, exiting with its status.
.B \-t
.I seconds
sets a time limit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH STARTUP FILES
On macOS, a login shell first adds the directories listed in
.I /etc/paths
and
.I /etc/paths.d
to
.BR PATH ,
as
.BR path_helper (8)
does for other shells. A login shell then runs
.I ~/.config/grfsh/profile
or
.IR ~/.grfsh_profile .
Shells that read commands from a terminal or standard input then run
.I ~/.config/grfsh/grfshrc
or
.IR ~/.grfshrc ,
and interactive shells load every
.I init.grfsh
found under
.IR ~/.config/grfsh/plugins/ .
.PP
A startup file or plugin is skipped with a warning when it, or the directory
holding it, belongs to another user or can be written by group or others.
.SH PROMPT
Run
.B configure\-prompt
to choose a theme with live previews, or
.B configure\-prompt \-\-preset
.I name
to pick one of
.BR lean ,
.BR classic ,
.BR rainbow ,
.BR pure " or " minimal .
The result is written to
.IR ~/.config/grfsh/theme ,
a plain
.I key = value
file that is reread when it changes. Without a theme file the
.B GRFSH_PROMPT
variable customizes the default prompt.
.PP
Functions named
.BR precmd ,
.BR preexec " and " chpwd ,
or listed in the arrays
.BR precmd_functions ,
.BR preexec_functions " and " chpwd_functions ,
run before each prompt, before each command line and after each directory
change.
.SH BUILTINS
Run
.B help
for the full list with descriptions and
.B help
.I name
for the usage of one builtin. The builtins are
.BR . ,
.BR : ,
.BR [ ,
.BR abbr ,
.BR alias ,
.BR bg ,
.BR bindkey ,
.BR break ,
.BR cd ,
.BR command ,
.BR complete ,
.BR configure\-prompt ,
.BR continue ,
.BR declare ,
.BR dirs ,
.BR echo ,
.BR eval ,
.BR exec ,
.BR exit ,
.BR explain ,
.BR export ,
.BR false ,
.BR fg ,
.BR getopts ,
.BR help ,
.BR history ,
.BR jobs ,
.BR kill ,
.BR local ,
.BR mapfile ,
.BR popd ,
.BR printf ,
.BR pushd ,
.BR pwd ,
.BR read ,
.BR readarray ,
.BR readonly ,
.BR replace ,
.BR return ,
.BR set ,
.BR shift ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR source ,
.BR test ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR trap ,
.BR true ,
.BR type ,
.BR typeset ,
.BR ulimit ,
.BR umask ,
.BR unalias ,
.BR unset ,
.BR wait ,
.B write\-file
and
.BR z .
.TP
.BI "timeout \fR[\fP\-s " signal "\fR] [\fP\-k " duration "\fR]\fP " "duration command"
Run a command, function or builtin in its own process group and signal the
whole group when the time runs out. Returns 124 after a timeout.
.TP
.BI "replace \fR[\fP\-\-all\fR] [\fP\-n\fR]\fP " "file old new"
Replace text that must appear exactly once, writing the file atomically.
With only a file, read blocks marked by lines starting with
.BR <<<<<<< ,
.B =======
and
.B >>>>>>>
from standard input. With only
.I old
and
.I new
and a pipe on standard input, replace on the lines listed by
.BR search .
.B \-n
shows the change without writing. Text with line feeds also matches files
with CRLF line endings, which are kept, and a block with an empty new side
deletes the old lines.
.TP
.BI "write\-file \fR[\fP\-p\fR] [\fP\-a\fR] [\fP\-f\fR]\fP " file
Write standard input to a file atomically. Empty input is refused, so a
missing pipe cannot empty a file, unless
.B \-f
is given.
.SH TABLE COMMANDS
.TP
.BR files " [path] [\-a] [\-r]"
List a directory as a table with name, type, size and modified columns.
.TP
.B procs
List running processes.
.TP
.BI "search \fR[\fP\-i\fR] [\fP\-F\fR] [\fP\-l\fR] [\fP\-g " glob "\fR]\fP " "pattern \fR[\fPpath...\fR]\fP"
Search file contents for an extended regular expression in which
.BR \ed ,
.BR \ew ,
.BR \es ,
.B \eb
and their upper case negations work on every system, skipping hidden
files, binary files and anything ignored by
.I .gitignore
files. Prints
.I path:line:text
to programs and files, and a table otherwise.
.TP
.B git\-status
List changed files with their state.
.TP
.B ports
List listening TCP ports and the processes that own them.
.TP
.BI load " file"
Read a csv, tsv, json or jsonl file, or any other file as lines.
.TP
.BR from " csv|tsv|json|jsonl|ws|lines"
Parse text from a pipe into a table.
.TP
.BI where " column op value"
Keep rows that match. Operators are
.BR == ", " != ", " =~ ", " !~ ", " has ", " lacks ", " eq ", " ne ", " lt ", " le ", " gt " and " ge ,
and conditions can be joined with
.BR and " and " or .
.B =~
and
.B !~
take an extended regular expression,
.B has
and
.B lacks
a substring in any case. Values follow the column type, so sizes accept
units such as
.B 10mb
and dates accept ages such as
.BR 2d ,
which means two days ago, and a value that does not fit is an error.
.TP
.BR sort\-by ", " select ", " reject ", " first ", " skip ", " reverse ", " count ", " get ", " group\-by ", " uniq\-by ", " math ", " update ", " to
Reshape, aggregate or print tables. See
.BR help .
.B get \-0
separates values with NUL bytes. Tables printed as text show control
characters as escapes such as
.B \ee
and
.BR \en .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CDPATH
Directories searched by
.B cd
for relative names.
.TP
.B GRFSH_PROMPT
Template for the default prompt when no theme file exists.
.TP
.B GRFSH_THEME
Name of a theme preset to use when no theme file exists.
.TP
.B GRFSH_AGENT
When set to 1, run in agent mode.
.TP
.B GRFSH_OUTPUT_LINES
Output limit in agent mode, 400 by default, 0 for no limit.
.TP
.B IFS
Field separators used for word splitting and
.BR read .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to a non empty value, disables all colors.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME
Base directories for configuration and the command log.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.grfsh_history
Line editor history, mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.grfsh_dirs
Directory visit counts used by
.BR z .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/grfsh/commands.tsv
Command log with time, duration, status and directory, read by
.BR "history \-\-table" .
.TP
.I ~/.config/grfsh/theme
Prompt theme.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The status of the last command run, 2 for a syntax error, 126 when a command
cannot be executed, 127 when a command is not found, and 128 plus the signal
number when a command is killed by a signal.
.SH SEE ALSO
The full documentation is at https://grf.sh/docs/
.PP
.BR bash (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR starship (1)
